[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 968,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 733,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137223,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 733,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epidermal inclusion cyst (EIC) is a common, benign cyst lined by epidermis with a formed granular cell layer, containing flaky, basket-weave, or epidermal-type keratin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 969,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 734,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137224,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137225,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EK70.0Z Epidermoid cyst, unspecified</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137226,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> epidermal cyst; epidermoid cyst.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137227,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137228,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cysts are most frequently found on the scalp and face, and they may occur along the eyelid margin in association with the cilia, along the brow, or elsewhere on the eyelid in association with the follicular units of vellus hairs.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137229,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EIC is typically a slow-growing, well-circumscribed, firm, and mobile dermal or subcutaneous mass. A central pore may be visible clinically {3341987}. Imaging has limited value in many eyelid lesions, but CT and MRI can confirm the diagnosis and delineate the extent of large subcutaneous epidermoid cysts {31516916}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137230,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EICs may occur in adolescents to elderly patients, without sex predilection. Occasional epidermoid cysts are congenital.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137231,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EICs are believed to arise from the infundibular portion of the pilosebaceous unit or from traumatic implantation of surface epidermis into the dermis/subcutis {23330009}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137232,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pathogenesis is typically unknown but may result from follicular occlusion. Multiple epidermal cysts may occasionally be associated with Gardner or Muir&ndash;Torre syndromes {3341987}. Rarely, EICs have been associated with medications, including BRAF inhibitors (e.g. vemurafenib) {23406731}, cyclosporin&nbsp;A, and tacrolimus {32043723}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137233,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EICs appears as well-circumscribed, white to yellow, oval to round masses filled with caseous or paste-like keratinous contents and occasionally hair shafts.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137234,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EICs are lined by stratified squamous epithelium identical to that of the surface epidermis and follicular infundibulum. A well-developed stratum granulosum is present, unlike the lining of pilar cyst. Contents consist of abundant flakes of laminated orthokeratin.</p>\r\n<p>The differential diagnosis includes dilated pore of Winer, dermoid cyst, pilar (trichilemmal) cyst, vellus hair cyst, comedonal cyst, milium or hybrid cyst (mixed hidrocystoma and EIC; see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/65/37\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Ductal hidrocystoma</em>).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137235,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 744,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FNAB or impression cytology is not typically used to make the diagnosis of this entity at this site. When performed, aspirates show many nucleate and anucleate mature squamous cells and keratinaceous debris {18855902}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 980,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 745,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137236,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 745,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 983,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 748,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137237,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 748,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a cyst in the dermis or subcutis; lined by epidermis with a granular cell layer; containing epidermal-type keratin; without an associated atypical squamous proliferation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 981,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 746,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137238,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 746,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 982,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 747,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137239,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 39,
        "headingId": 747,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Complete excision is typically curative. Rare cases of carcinoma (squamous or basal cell carcinoma) arising within an EIC have been reported {22460681;20736874}, and cysts may become secondarily infected {21158377}. Rupture without infection is common {23330009}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]